    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was to expose a derived `timezone` field on the owner, mapping the locality/region to an IANA name via the fixed table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane).\n\nFollowing the existing `locality` pattern, I made two changes:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `timezone` (read-only string) property to the `Owner` schema, right after `locality`. The DTO (`OwnerDto`) is code-generated from this spec at build time.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"timezone\", expression = \"java(timezone(owner))\")` and a `timezone(Owner)` method backed by a fixed `REGION_TIMEZONES` table. It reuses the existing `localityRegion(owner)` helper (the same region source that drives `locality`), so the two fields stay consistent, and returns `null` (field absent) when the owner has no known region.\n\nOne subtlety I handled: `Map.of(...).get(null)` throws `NullPointerException`, so `timezone()` guards for a null region before the lookup rather than passing it straight through.\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the acceptance test asserts both `$.locality == \"NSW\"` and `$.timezone == \"Australia/Sydney\"`).",
